\title[The order-free triangle]{The Order-Free Triangle: Large-Order Elements Cannot Move the Exponent One-Fifth in Harvey's Deterministic Factoring Algorithm}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Harvey's deterministic integer factorisation algorithm runs in time $O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N)$, the best known rigorous deterministic bound. Its analysis balances three costs: the number $r$ of Lehman candidate pairs, the size $m$ of a baby-step table, and the residual giant-step work $N^{1/2}/(r^{1/2}m)$. The baby-step table is built from powers of an element $\alpha\in(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^\times$, so its size is limited by the multiplicative order of $\alpha$. Recent deterministic methods for producing elements of large multiplicative order raised the question of whether such an element could rebalance the trade-off and beat the exponent $1/5$. We show that it cannot within Harvey's cost model, and we quantify why.
(i) The exponent $1/5$ is certified by an \emph{order-free} linear certificate with weights $(1/5,2/5,2/5)$, and the minimiser is unique.
(ii) Under the correctness constraint $m\le\ord_N(\alpha)=N^\omega$, the optimum is exactly $\max\{1/5,(1-2\omega)/3\}$, so the constraint has zero shadow price once $\omega\ge 1/5$.
(iii) With all logarithmic factors retained, the running time $T(r,m)=(\sqrt N/(\sqrt r\,m)+r)L^4+mL^2$ satisfies $T\ge F:=N^{1/5}L^{16/5}$, and $T=3F$ is attained. Any nonnegative order-dependent additive term leaves the lower bound intact.
(iv) Producing an element of order $N^\omega$ at cost $N^{\omega/2}$ is dominated everywhere.
(v) A filter on Lehman's pair family with compression $N^\sigma$ yields exactly $1/5-3\sigma/5$ if it is generative and $1/5-2\sigma/5$ if it is evaluated. A sound residue sieve on residue-uniform good pairs is trivial.
(vi) If $(p-1)\mid(q-1)$, then $\gcd(x^{pq-1}-1,pq)=p$ whenever $q\nmid x^{pq-1}-1$.
Throughout, $N^{1/5}$ is an upper bound on the deterministic state of the art, not a proven lower bound for factoring.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Deterministic factoring}
Let $N=pq$ be a product of two primes. The fastest practical factoring algorithms have heuristic subexponential running times, and rigorous randomised subexponential algorithms are also known. For algorithms that are both \emph{deterministic} and \emph{rigorously analysed}, however, the best known bounds are of the form $N^{c+o(1)}$. For decades the record was $c=1/4$, from the Pollard--Strassen method, later improved by logarithmic factors. In 2020 Harvey obtained $c=1/5$, with running time $O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N)$. His algorithm combines Lehman's method, which reduces factoring to a search over small integer pairs $(a,b)$, with a baby-step giant-step search modulo $N$.

\subsection{Where the multiplicative order enters}
The baby-step table consists of the consecutive powers $\alpha,\alpha^2,\dots,\alpha^m$ of some $\alpha\in(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^\times$. These are pairwise distinct if and only if $m\le\ord_N(\alpha)$, and the bound is sharp. Thus the order of $\alpha$ enters in exactly two ways:
\begin{enumerate}
\item as a \emph{correctness constraint} $m\le\ord_N(\alpha)$;
\item through the number of \emph{spurious hash-table hits} fed to a product-tree computation. This is a nonnegative additive term, polylogarithmic at the balanced parameters.
\end{enumerate}
It does not enter the Lehman pair budget $r$, the baby-step budget $m$, or the smoothness-sieve range $\lceil (N/r)^{1/2}\rceil$.

Gao, Feng, Hu and Pan give a deterministic procedure that, given $N$ and $\delta$ with $N^{1/4}\log^8N\le\delta\le N$, returns an element of order greater than $\delta$ (or a factor of $N$, or a primality certificate) in time $O(\delta^{1/2}\log^2N/(\log\log\delta)^{1/2})$. Harvey and Hittmeir gave further deterministic methods for finding elements of large multiplicative order. This raises the question: \emph{with hypothesis-free order-finding, can Harvey's trade-off be rebalanced to beat $N^{1/5}$?} We show that it cannot.

\section{The cost model}

Let $L=\lgN$. Harvey's analysis bounds the running time, up to a constant factor, by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:T}
T(r,m)=\Bigl(\frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m}+r\Bigr)L^4+mL^2 .
\end{equation}
The three summands are the \emph{pair leg} $X=rL^4$ (the family $\mathcal P_r=\{(a,b):ab\le r\}$ has $\Theta(r\log r)$ elements), the \emph{giant-step interior} $Y=N^{1/2}L^4/(r^{1/2}m)$, and the \emph{baby-step leg} $Z=mL^2$.

\begin{definition}[Exponent cost]
With $r=N^a$ and $m=N^b$, define $c(a,b)=\max\{a,\ b,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Order-constrained value]
For $\ord_N(\alpha)=N^\omega$, define $V(\omega)=\max\{\tfrac15,\ \tfrac{1-2\omega}{3}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Filtered costs]
For a compression exponent $\sigma$, define
\[
c^{\mathrm{gen}}_\sigma(a,b)=\max\{a-\sigma,\ b,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-\sigma\},\qquad
c^{\mathrm{eval}}_\sigma(a,b)=\max\{a,\ b,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-\sigma\}.
\]
A \emph{generative} filter outputs only the surviving pairs, in time proportional to their number. An \emph{evaluated} filter must examine every pair before discarding it.
\end{definition}

\section{The order-free triangle}

\begin{theorem}[Order-free certificate; unique minimiser]\label{thm:cert}
For all real $a,b$ we have $c(a,b)\ge 1/5$, with equality if and only if $a=b=1/5$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The weighted combination of the legs with weights $(\tfrac15,\tfrac25,\tfrac25)$ is identically constant:
\[
\tfrac15a+\tfrac25b+\tfrac25\bigl(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\bigr)=\tfrac15 .
\]
A maximum dominates every convex combination of its arguments, so $c(a,b)\ge1/5$. If $c(a,b)=1/5$, then every leg is at most $1/5$ while their positive-weighted average equals $1/5$. Hence all legs equal $1/5$, and in particular $a=b=1/5$. The converse is a direct check.
\end{proof}

No leg involves the order of $\alpha$: the triangle fixing $1/5$ is order-free.

\section{The order is a constraint, not a lever}

\begin{theorem}[Order-constrained optimum]\label{thm:order}
For every real $\omega$: \textup{(1)} $V(\omega)\le c(a,b)$ for all $a$ and all $b\le\omega$; and \textup{(2)} there exist $a$ and $b\le\omega$ with $c(a,b)=V(\omega)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} gives $c\ge1/5$. With the weights $(\tfrac13,0,\tfrac23)$ we get $3c(a,b)\ge a+2(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)=1-2b\ge1-2\omega$.
(2) If $\omega\ge1/5$, take $a=b=1/5$. Otherwise take $b=\omega$ and $a=(1-2\omega)/3$. Then the third leg equals $(1-2\omega)/3$, and $b=\omega<(1-2\omega)/3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Threshold and zero shadow price]
$V(\omega)=1/5$ if and only if $\omega\ge1/5$, and $V(\omega)>1/5$ if $\omega<1/5$. If $\omega_1,\omega_2\ge 1/5$ then $V(\omega_1)=V(\omega_2)$. In particular, an element of maximal order is worth exactly as much as one of order $N^{1/5}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Order is not a lever]
If $b\le\omega$ then $c(a,b)\ge1/5$, and $c(a,b)=1/5$ forces $\omega\ge1/5$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By uniqueness in Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}, $c=1/5$ forces $b=1/5\le\omega$.
\end{proof}

\section{Retaining the logarithmic factors}

\begin{lemma}[Weighted product identity]\label{lem:prod}
For $N\ge0$ and $r,m>0$ we have $XY^2Z^2=NL^{16}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$rL^4\cdot\dfrac{NL^8}{rm^2}\cdot m^2L^4=NL^{16}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Polylogarithmic lower bound and attainment]\label{thm:poly}
Let $F=N^{1/5}L^{16/5}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For all $N,L\ge0$ and $r,m>0$, we have $F\le T(r,m)$.
\item Write $N=u^{10}$ and $L=\ell^5$ with $u,\ell>0$, so that $F=u^2\ell^{16}$. At $r^\ast=u^2/\ell^4=N^{1/5}L^{-4/5}$ and $m^\ast=u^2\ell^6=N^{1/5}L^{6/5}$, each summand equals $F$, so $T(r^\ast,m^\ast)=3F$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) By weighted AM--GM and Lemma~\ref{lem:prod},
\[
T=X+Y+Z\ \ge\ \tfrac15X+\tfrac25Y+\tfrac25Z\ \ge\ X^{1/5}Y^{2/5}Z^{2/5}=(NL^{16})^{1/5}=F.
\]
(2) Here $\sqrt N=u^5$ and $\sqrt{r^\ast}=u/\ell^2$. Then $X=u^2\ell^{16}$, $Z=u^2\ell^{16}$, and $Y=u^5\ell^{20}/(u^3\ell^4)=u^2\ell^{16}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Free order-finding cannot help]
For any additional order-dependent cost $P\ge0$, we have $F\le T(r,m)+P$ for all $r,m>0$. With $P=0$ the optimum lies in $[F,3F]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Beyond the constraint, the order affects only the spurious hit count, a quantity with $N$-exponent $0$. The target quantity has $N$-exponent $1/5$. The corollary is the precise form of this mismatch.
\end{remark}

\section{Producing large-order elements is dominated}

\begin{theorem}[Production is dominated]
\textup{(1)} For all $a$ and $b\ge0$, $\max\{c(a,b),b/2\}=c(a,b)$.
\textup{(2)} For all $a,b,\omega$ with $b\le\omega$, $\max\{c(a,b),\omega/2\}\ge1/5$, with equality at $a=b=\omega=1/5$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) $b/2\le b\le c(a,b)$. (2) The bound follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}. At the given point the value is $\max\{1/5,1/10\}=1/5$.
\end{proof}
For $\delta=N^{1/3}$ the production cost is $N^{1/6}$, and $1/6<1/5$.

\section{Filtering Lehman's pairs}

\begin{theorem}[Filter values]\label{thm:filter}
For every real $\sigma$: $\min c^{\mathrm{gen}}_\sigma=\tfrac15-\tfrac{3\sigma}5$ and $\min c^{\mathrm{eval}}_\sigma=\tfrac15-\tfrac{2\sigma}5$, and both minima are attained. Hence the exponent $1/6$ requires $\sigma=1/18$ (generative) or $\sigma=1/12$ (evaluated).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The weights $(\tfrac15,\tfrac25,\tfrac25)$ give the identities
$\tfrac15(a-\sigma)+\tfrac25b+\tfrac25(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-\sigma)=\tfrac15-\tfrac{3\sigma}5$ and
$\tfrac15a+\tfrac25b+\tfrac25(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b-\sigma)=\tfrac15-\tfrac{2\sigma}5$.
The minima are attained at $b=\tfrac15-\tfrac{3\sigma}5$, $a=b+\sigma$ and at $a=b=\tfrac15-\tfrac{2\sigma}5$ respectively.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sound residue sieves are trivial]
Let $g:I\to\mathbb Z/\ell\mathbb Z$ assign to each instance the residue class of its good pair, and suppose $g$ is surjective. If $A\subseteq\mathbb Z/\ell\mathbb Z$ contains $g(i)$ for every $i$, then $A=\mathbb Z/\ell\mathbb Z$ and $|A|=\ell$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every class has the form $g(i)$ for some $i$, and $g(i)\in A$.
\end{proof}
Empirically, over random balanced semiprimes, the residues of $ab$ for the good pair fill every class modulo $3,5,7,11$.

\section{The large Euler gap}

\begin{lemma}[Exponent reduction]
For a prime $p$, an integer $q\ge1$ and $a\in\mathbb F_p^\times$, we have $a^{pq-1}=a^{q-1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$pq-1=(q-1)+q(p-1)$, and $a^{p-1}=1$ by Fermat's little theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Euler-gap split]
Let $p,q$ be primes with $(p-1)\mid(q-1)$, and let $x\ge1$ satisfy $p\nmid x$ and $q\nmid x^{pq-1}-1$. Then $\gcd(x^{pq-1}-1,pq)=p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the lemma, $x^{pq-1}\equiv x^{q-1}\equiv1\pmod p$, so $p\mid g:=\gcd(x^{pq-1}-1,pq)$. Write $g=pk$. Then $k\mid q$. The case $k=q$ would give $q\mid x^{pq-1}-1$, so $k=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$341=11\cdot31$ is the smallest base-$2$ Fermat pseudoprime, and $10\mid 30$. Base $2$ gives $2^{340}\equiv1$ modulo both primes. Base $3$ gives $\gcd(3^{340}-1,341)=11$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textbf{A.}] \emph{Order-constrained optimiser:} if $\omega\ge1/5$ return $(1/5,1/5)$; otherwise return $((1-2\omega)/3,\omega)$.
\item[\textbf{B.}] \emph{Balanced parameters:} $r^\ast=N^{1/5}L^{-4/5}$ and $m^\ast=N^{1/5}L^{6/5}$, giving $T=3F$.
\item[\textbf{C.}] \emph{Filter planner:} generative: $b=\tfrac15-\tfrac{3\sigma}5$, $a=b+\sigma$; evaluated: $a=b=\tfrac15-\tfrac{2\sigma}5$.
\item[\textbf{D.}] \emph{Euler-gap splitter:} for $x=2,\dots,B$, test whether $g=\gcd(x^{N-1}-1\bmod N,N)$ satisfies $1<g<N$. This costs $B$ modular exponentiations.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
All results concern the cost structure of Harvey's algorithm and natural modifications of it. They give no lower bound for factoring: no unconditional superpolynomial lower bound is known in any standard model. Two failure modes are separated. The large-order lever acts on the \emph{wrong quantity}: an $N^0$ hit count and an already-slack constraint. Residue sieves act on the \emph{right quantity}, the pair family, but compress by a factor $1$. Rational base descent gives an $O(\log^3N)$ split when $p$ is close to $c(a/b)^n$; precisely, with $\Delta=p-\lfloor c(a/b)^n\rfloor$, a split occurs when $(a/b)^n>q$ and $|\Delta|<(a/b)^n/q$. This is a promise class, so it gives no new exponent for balanced semiprimes. A conjecture on sparse sets $S,T$ (of size $n^\beta$, with entries bounded by $e^{n^\alpha}$) whose differences cover all divisors $i\le n$ would imply exponent $1/6$, and remains a conditional route outside the order lever.

\section{Future work}
\begin{conjecture}[Generative pair-filter threshold]
Any deterministic generator that outputs a subfamily of $\{(a,b):ab\le r\}$ guaranteed to contain the good pair, in time proportional to its output, has output size $r^{1-o(1)}$ for worst-case balanced semiprimes.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Joint shadow prices]
With a generative filter $\sigma$ and the constraint $b\le\omega$, the optimum is $\max\{1/5-3\sigma/5,(1-2\omega-3\sigma)/3\}$, and the order threshold is $\omega^\ast=1/5-3\sigma/5$.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Hit-count law]
The expected number of spurious hash hits is $\Theta(sm/D)$, polylogarithmic in $N$ at the balanced point, uniformly over elements of order at least $N^{1/5}$.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Euler-gap density]
Among balanced semiprimes $N\le X$, those with $\gcd(p-1,q-1)\ge N^\theta$ have density $X^{-\theta+o(1)}$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}
Harvey's $1/5$ comes from an order-free three-leg balance. The order of the baby-step element is a constraint that becomes slack at $\omega=1/5$, plus a nonnegative term that cannot lower the bound. The lever for progress is the pair family, with exact exchange rates of $3/5$ (generative) and $2/5$ (evaluated) per unit of compression exponent.

